# Credentials

Check where you run first: `test -n "$OP_CONNECT_HOST" && echo imp || echo local`. In both places, never
echo a token or a secret value.

## Local PC (1Password, scoped tokens per repo)

- Use the Linux `op` CLI with a service-account token that `op-mint` scopes to one profile's vaults.
- Each repo has its own profile and its own vault, both named after the repo. The profile's token
  lives in `~/projects/<repo>/.claude/settings.local.json`, which overrides the global
  `~/.claude/settings.json`. `op-mint --list` gives every profile, its vaults and its settings file.
- These profiles differ from the convention:
  - `global` uses the `zgeoff` vault, in `~/.claude/settings.json`. `zgeoff` holds the items every
    agent shares, such as `repo-sync-github-app`.
  - `imp` uses the `imp-e2e` vault, which holds imp's e2e tailnet OAuth client.
  - `vers` adds `vers-ci` (read-only), which holds only what the vers infra-drift CI job reads.
  - `cloud` adds `cloud-deploy` (deploy secrets), `imp` (the files service's agent token) and
    `fleet` (Claude slot tokens).
- My personal vaults are reachable only through `op-win`, with my approval.
- Claude Code loads the token from `env.OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN`, so plain `op item get …` works
  there. In any other agent, or in a Claude session started before a re-mint, read it inline:
  `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(jq -r .env.OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN <settings file>) op …`.
- Tokens expire ~7d. When an `op` call fails auth, re-mint with `op-mint <profile>` (dotfiles
  `~/.local/bin/op-mint`; `op-mint --list` shows the profiles). Don't ask me to sign in the Linux CLI;
  `op-mint` mints through `op-win`, which reaches my Windows 1Password app; approving may need a tap.
  A new profile or vault means editing `op-mint`, not minting by hand.
- Each mint makes a new `claude-<profile>-<date>` service account — prune stale ones in the
  1Password web UI occasionally.

## Cloud (imp)

- No `op-mint`, `op-win` or service-account token exists here. Don't look for one.
- `op read op://imp/<item>/<field>` works through 1Password Connect, for the `imp` vault only.
- Brokered APIs need no key in the guest: the host adds the credential on the way out. Today that
  covers GitHub and the model gateways; call them directly.
- If a secret you need isn't reachable, report the gap. Never ask me to paste a secret into the
  session.

# GitHub

- Agents act on GitHub with my own `zgeoff` login and keys.
- By default, work on a branch and open a PR. Push straight to `main` only when I say so. On either path, get the one review that "Building and reviewing" asks for before you hand off or push.
- Never use `gh pr merge --admin` or any other ruleset bypass.
- Open the PR with the repo's PR template. Keep the description short: a lead of 2 sentences or fewer, one-line bullets, 150 words or fewer. Write the short version first.
- After pushing, link the PR URL in your reply.

# Working on Linear tasks

When working on a Linear task, include its issue ID in the PR description with an explicit relationship:

- Fixes GEO-123: merging satisfies the task’s completion criteria.
- Contributes to GEO-123: delivers part of the task; work remains.
- Related to GEO-123: context only; no status changes.

Name the issue only in the PR body. Never put it in the PR title or the branch name: Linear closes an issue that a merged PR names there. In a PR that only records a grant, write Related to.

Verify the PR is linked in Linear. Don’t use Fixes while required evaluation, deployment or acceptance work remains.

# Stack taste

- Prefer Bun, Elysia, Kysely and TanStack (Start/Query). Use a typed API layer (oRPC-class); never hand-roll REST.
- Avoid Next.js, Remix / React Router framework mode, and Drizzle.
- Adopt TypeScript 7 / tsgo eagerly, RCs included. Don't propose "safe" holdbacks.
- In personal projects, pick interesting over safe. Experimental APIs are welcome.

# Building and reviewing

PRs get a different review depending on what they change:

- **Any PR, by default:** one review before hand-off. When you built it through build-feature with
  its Codex review (`review: 'codex'`), that review counts, even when it fell back to another model.
  Otherwise get one through the `second-opinion` skill. The PR does not merge until every finding is
  fixed or answered.
- **An update bot's patch, security or minor update, or its lock-file refresh:** merges on green CI,
  with no local review. A security update that is a major bump of another package follows the major
  bump rule below.
- **An update bot's bump of my own packages** (any package built from a `github.com/zgeoff` repo,
  or the `zgeoff/tools` sync ref), at any version level: merges on green CI, with no local review.
- **An update bot's major bump of any other package:** one local review before hand-off, through
  the `second-opinion` skill.

Review rounds:

- Evidence, not votes. On disagreement, write the discriminating test. Do not re-prompt for a
  preferred answer.
- Fix every finding, and every defect you meet while doing the work, in the same PR, whatever its
  size. File a follow-up issue only for a fix in another repo, or one that needs my decision.
- In a re-check, check only the fixes, and raise no new findings.
- Fix a security defect before merge.
- Report a timeout on a required review; never skip it silently.
